#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "509fc92e-cf26-503c-bb89-8df87012e393" :question/math-academy-id "e-5767" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6x^4-7x^3+11x-9,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm1,$ $\\pm3,$ and $\\pm9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\; \\pm \\frac13, \\; \\pm \\frac12, \\; \\pm 1, \\; \\pm \\frac32, \\; \\pm 3, \\; \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, \\; \\pm 9.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm$ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2 \\cdot 8 = 16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "12016ef4-6119-543c-adda-aa30fa7a73ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7365" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 12x^4+5x^3-6x+15?$\n\nI. $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\dfrac{3}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $15$ and the leading coefficient is $12.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{15}}{\\textrm{factor of }12}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\dfrac 23$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "20d00218-7823-5ef5-b196-a02894edc69a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7378" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 9x^4-3x^2-7x-6?$\n\nI. $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\nIII. $-9$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $9.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{-6}}{\\textrm{factor of }9}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c9c0d2-6af5-520a-8b56-4930780bc6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7413" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec68e86-8f08-510f-8171-dc1ae0f4f3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399776} {:answer/id #uuid "1000ee85-77e8-514d-87b6-5fa6ba7c90fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399777} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7fad0-aec1-568c-9216-1773d3768409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399778} {:answer/id #uuid "62c60f36-b157-50e0-b5d9-80c8be74ad99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399779} {:answer/id #uuid "1e872297-3c68-5e8b-85d5-47d2da2edc63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e872297-3c68-5e8b-85d5-47d2da2edc63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399780} :answer-field/i